1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:01,641
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01,787 --> 00:00:03,218
My name is Jaimin Sutrawala.

3
00:00:03,687 --> 00:00:07,643
I'm the associate director of CRM Strategy
and Operations at United Therapeutics.

4
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:12,793
Today I'm going to talk about de-risking
a legacy CRM migration in life sciences.

5
00:00:13,601 --> 00:00:14,934
So let me start with a claim.

6
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:18,092
The riskiest feature you will
ever release isn't a new checkout

7
00:00:18,096 --> 00:00:19,921
flow or a redesigned dashboard.

8
00:00:20,431 --> 00:00:21,611
It's an entire platform.

9
00:00:22,095 --> 00:00:26,262
Think about what a CRM migration
actually ships on a go-live day: a new

10
00:00:26,282 --> 00:00:31,311
data model, new identity rules, new
integrations, new communication workflows,

11
00:00:31,686 --> 00:00:35,906
new permissions, and very often, all
of it switches on at once on a single

12
00:00:35,928 --> 00:00:38,644
weekend for every user and every record.

13
00:00:39,486 --> 00:00:42,632
We would never release an application
feature that way, so why do we

14
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:44,212
keep releasing platforms that way?

15
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:48,809
I have spent a good part of my career
on large CRM and digital platform

16
00:00:48,811 --> 00:00:52,679
transformations in regulated life
science environments, where the

17
00:00:52,679 --> 00:00:55,983
records describe real people and
the messages reach real people.

18
00:00:56,438 --> 00:01:00,446
And the most useful lesson I have
taken from that work is this: the

19
00:01:00,446 --> 00:01:04,807
technique that-- the techniques this
community uses every day, feature flags,

20
00:01:04,925 --> 00:01:10,062
canaries, progressive rollout, scale up
remarkably well to platform migration.

21
00:01:10,679 --> 00:01:14,362
So for the next twenty minutes,
let's treat a CRM migration as a

22
00:01:14,362 --> 00:01:16,802
feature release and see what changes

23
00:01:17,802 --> 00:01:19,867
So why are CRM migrations different?

24
00:01:20,216 --> 00:01:22,510
Because the CRM is really
just an application.

25
00:01:22,896 --> 00:01:24,943
It's-- In most enterprises, it's a hub.

26
00:01:25,511 --> 00:01:26,783
Look at what hangs off it.

27
00:01:27,236 --> 00:01:31,958
Identity, which record is, is which
person, and how duplicates gets merged.

28
00:01:32,358 --> 00:01:37,808
Integrations, web forms, support programs,
contact centers, data warehouses, partner

29
00:01:37,811 --> 00:01:43,752
feeds, communications, emails, text,
reminders, all triggered by CRM state.

30
00:01:44,261 --> 00:01:48,624
Consent, who agreed to hear from you
about what through which channel?

31
00:01:49,063 --> 00:01:52,077
And downstream analytics that
people open every morning without

32
00:01:52,096 --> 00:01:53,710
knowing where the numbers come from.

33
00:01:54,539 --> 00:01:56,872
In li-- in life sciences,
add one more layer.

34
00:01:57,113 --> 00:01:59,895
The data often includes personal
and health-related information,

35
00:02:00,264 --> 00:02:02,553
and the processes around it
are governed and audited.

36
00:02:03,265 --> 00:02:04,842
Here is the uncomfortable part.

37
00:02:05,393 --> 00:02:08,394
Most of these dependencies
aren't documented in one place.

38
00:02:08,768 --> 00:02:14,113
They live in field names, in nightly
jobs, in a workflow someone built years

39
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:16,614
ago that nobody remembers until it stops.

40
00:02:17,132 --> 00:02:20,985
So the real question isn't
just will the new system work?

41
00:02:21,448 --> 00:02:22,684
It's the blast radius.

42
00:02:23,183 --> 00:02:27,741
When something goes wrong, how many
people, records, and processes does

43
00:02:27,741 --> 00:02:30,307
it touch before anyone notices?

44
00:02:31,307 --> 00:02:33,727
The traditional answer
is the Big Bang cutover.

45
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:41,064
Freeze legacy on Friday, migrate the data,
repoint every integration, move the users,

46
00:02:41,674 --> 00:02:43,638
switch on the new communication journeys.

47
00:02:43,981 --> 00:02:46,566
By Monday, the old system
is read-only or gone.

48
00:02:47,179 --> 00:02:48,286
On paper, it's clean.

49
00:02:48,664 --> 00:02:50,634
One date, one plan, one go live.

50
00:02:51,196 --> 00:02:52,885
But look at what is really happening.

51
00:02:53,456 --> 00:02:59,619
Four kinds of change: data, integrations,
users, and communications all

52
00:02:59,622 --> 00:03:01,274
cross the line at the same moment.

53
00:03:02,078 --> 00:03:05,128
That's the opposite of everything
we have learned about safe releases.

54
00:03:05,649 --> 00:03:08,541
We have maximized the size of
the change and minimized our

55
00:03:08,541 --> 00:03:10,147
ability to isolate a problem.

56
00:03:10,873 --> 00:03:12,219
And then there is rollback.

57
00:03:13,182 --> 00:03:15,213
Rolling back application code is easy.

58
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:20,076
Rolling back a platform migration
means unmigrating all data that

59
00:03:20,076 --> 00:03:24,128
has already changed since Monday,
working out which emails already

60
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:28,565
went out, repointing integrations
that have already written records.

61
00:03:29,034 --> 00:03:33,623
By Wednesday, rollback quietly stops
being an option, and the plan becomes

62
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:35,507
fix forward whatever it takes.

63
00:03:36,215 --> 00:03:37,130
That's the trap.

64
00:03:37,738 --> 00:03:40,600
Big Bang migrat- Big Bang
migrations don't always fail.

65
00:03:41,094 --> 00:03:44,870
Many succeed, but when they fail,
they fail with nowhere to go

66
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:49,361
Now, this audience already
knows what the way out of that

67
00:03:49,367 --> 00:03:51,748
trap, at least for applications.

68
00:03:52,397 --> 00:03:56,036
The most important idea in modern
delivery is that deployment and

69
00:03:56,037 --> 00:03:57,961
releases are different events.

70
00:03:58,569 --> 00:04:01,756
Deployment means the code or
configuration exists in production.

71
00:04:02,136 --> 00:04:06,117
Release means real users and real
traffic are actually exercising it.

72
00:04:06,896 --> 00:04:11,742
Pete Hodgson's article on Martin Fowler's
site frames feature toggles exactly that

73
00:04:11,754 --> 00:04:16,533
way, and James Governor gave the wider
practice a name, progressive delivery.

74
00:04:17,069 --> 00:04:21,241
Canaries, blue-green environments,
percentage rollouts, they all

75
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:23,624
separate it's there from it's on.

76
00:04:24,271 --> 00:04:26,470
So here is the question
for the rest of this talk.

77
00:04:26,972 --> 00:04:30,087
What if we apply the same
separation to a platform migration?

78
00:04:30,555 --> 00:04:34,923
The new CRM can be fully deployed,
configured, integrated, loaded

79
00:04:34,923 --> 00:04:38,530
with data, and still not be
the system anyone depends on.

80
00:04:39,184 --> 00:04:42,207
Activation then becomes a
series of decisions we control.

81
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:46,567
For which cohort, for which
capability, through which channel,

82
00:04:46,621 --> 00:04:48,815
and with what evidence that it's safe

83
00:04:49,815 --> 00:04:54,802
Here is the central idea, the diagram
I want, I most want you to remember.

84
00:04:55,653 --> 00:05:01,129
At the top is everything that touches
the CRM, internal users, the people

85
00:05:01,129 --> 00:05:04,638
whose records you hold, the channels
you communicate through, and the

86
00:05:04,639 --> 00:05:06,257
systems that integrate with you.

87
00:05:06,914 --> 00:05:11,153
At the bottom are two platforms that
coexist for a while, legacy and new.

88
00:05:11,816 --> 00:05:14,829
In between sits what I call
a migration control plane.

89
00:05:15,732 --> 00:05:18,893
Its job is to answer one
question consistently everywhere.

90
00:05:19,533 --> 00:05:24,305
For this request, this record, or this
workflow, which part do we take right now?

91
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:26,970
And the answer isn't one global switch.

92
00:05:27,462 --> 00:05:29,674
It is evaluated across dimensions.

93
00:05:30,291 --> 00:05:32,252
Which cohort is this record in?

94
00:05:32,438 --> 00:05:33,791
Which capability is involved?

95
00:05:34,215 --> 00:05:36,499
Profile updates or
outbound communications?

96
00:05:36,684 --> 00:05:37,442
Which channel?

97
00:05:37,594 --> 00:05:38,327
Which region?

98
00:05:38,694 --> 00:05:40,079
Which integration is calling?

99
00:05:40,627 --> 00:05:42,326
Which data domain is being touched?

100
00:05:42,733 --> 00:05:47,454
An important clarification, I'm not saying
you need a commercial fla- flag product

101
00:05:47,454 --> 00:05:49,412
wired into every corner of your CRM.

102
00:05:50,005 --> 00:05:53,549
Sometimes the control plane
is a flag service with an SDK.

103
00:05:54,049 --> 00:05:58,199
Sometimes it's routing rules in
a gateway, a configuration table

104
00:05:58,201 --> 00:06:01,874
your integration layer reads, or
eligibility rules on a segment.

105
00:06:02,388 --> 00:06:06,955
For the code-level piece, open
feature, the CNCF's vendor neutral

106
00:06:06,958 --> 00:06:11,289
flag API keeps your evaluation
logic from being tied to one vendor.

107
00:06:11,921 --> 00:06:13,737
The principle is what matters here.

108
00:06:14,391 --> 00:06:18,370
Externalize the rollout decision from
the code, govern it in one place,

109
00:06:18,402 --> 00:06:22,936
and make every decision controllable,
observable, auditable, and reversible.

110
00:06:23,318 --> 00:06:24,604
And notice the bottom band.

111
00:06:26,526 --> 00:06:31,101
Observability, reconciliation, and
audit are how the control plane knows

112
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:32,813
where its last decision was a good one

113
00:06:33,813 --> 00:06:36,986
Once you have a control
plane, you don't-- you need a

114
00:06:36,986 --> 00:06:38,555
vocabulary for where things are.

115
00:06:39,121 --> 00:06:44,159
This is the state model I have found
most useful, and it applies per cohort,

116
00:06:44,194 --> 00:06:48,134
per capability, or per integration,
not just to the whole program.

117
00:06:49,087 --> 00:06:49,986
Legacy only.

118
00:06:50,258 --> 00:06:51,342
Nothing has changed.

119
00:06:51,698 --> 00:06:54,399
The new platform may be
deployed, but it's dark.

120
00:06:54,936 --> 00:06:55,514
Shadow.

121
00:06:55,645 --> 00:06:59,558
The new platform receives real
inputs and processes them, but its

122
00:06:59,587 --> 00:07:01,442
output go nowhere that matters.

123
00:07:02,018 --> 00:07:02,690
Dual run.

124
00:07:03,062 --> 00:07:06,638
Both systems are fed, kept consistent,
and reconciled continuously.

125
00:07:07,087 --> 00:07:09,445
This is where the subtle
differences so-- show up.

126
00:07:10,026 --> 00:07:10,549
Pilot.

127
00:07:10,741 --> 00:07:14,455
For a small chosen cohort, the new
platform becomes the system of action.

128
00:07:15,134 --> 00:07:18,491
Real outcomes, limited blast
radius, legacy kept warm.

129
00:07:20,282 --> 00:07:21,096
New primary.

130
00:07:21,141 --> 00:07:22,683
The new platform is the default.

131
00:07:23,023 --> 00:07:26,746
Legacy still receives data
precisely so rollback stays cheap.

132
00:07:27,667 --> 00:07:28,663
Legacy disabled.

133
00:07:28,867 --> 00:07:32,610
The safety net comes down deliberately
after exit criteria are met.

134
00:07:33,294 --> 00:07:34,635
Two rules make this work.

135
00:07:35,349 --> 00:07:39,918
Every transition has clear as, as an
entry criteria and a defined way back,

136
00:07:40,544 --> 00:07:42,395
and the state is explicit and recorded.

137
00:07:42,917 --> 00:07:47,076
At any moment, you should be able
to ask, what state is this cohort

138
00:07:47,104 --> 00:07:49,621
in, since when, and who approved it?

139
00:07:50,028 --> 00:07:53,849
If you can't answer that, you
don't have a migration plan.

140
00:07:54,134 --> 00:07:54,873
You have hope.

141
00:07:55,873 --> 00:07:58,079
Pattern number one, cohort-based rollout.

142
00:07:58,654 --> 00:08:03,924
The familiar version is
percentages, five, 20, 50, 100.

143
00:08:04,418 --> 00:08:08,525
That works, and the gates between the
steps matter more than the numbers.

144
00:08:09,214 --> 00:08:12,575
But in an enterprise, in an
enterprise migration, a random

145
00:08:12,575 --> 00:08:15,065
percentage is often the wrong cohort.

146
00:08:15,742 --> 00:08:19,707
Random sampling spreads risk evenly,
which sounds fair, but it means

147
00:08:19,737 --> 00:08:23,389
every team gets a few odd records
and nobody can see a pattern.

148
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,232
One approach I have found particularly
useful is choosing cohorts

149
00:08:27,239 --> 00:08:33,416
deli-deliberately by internal team, so one
group learns the new tools together; by

150
00:08:33,430 --> 00:08:39,524
segment, so a well-understood population
goes first; by data complexity, clean

151
00:08:39,526 --> 00:08:43,371
recent records before the ones with
15 years of merges be-behind them;

152
00:08:44,022 --> 00:08:46,697
by workflow; by region; by channel.

153
00:08:47,307 --> 00:08:51,170
And a subtle one, make cohort
assignments sticky and deterministic.

154
00:08:51,776 --> 00:08:54,783
If a record bounces between
platforms from one day to the

155
00:08:54,783 --> 00:08:58,662
next, you create exactly the
inconsistencies you're trying to avoid.

156
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:04,377
Hash on a stable identifier, record the
assignment, and change it only on purpose

157
00:09:05,377 --> 00:09:08,240
Pattern number two: integration routing.

158
00:09:08,918 --> 00:09:13,872
In most CRM migrations, the scariest
moment isn't moving users, it's moving

159
00:09:13,872 --> 00:09:18,411
integrations because integrations write
data whether you are watching or not.

160
00:09:19,042 --> 00:09:21,024
So put a routing decision
in front of them.

161
00:09:21,491 --> 00:09:26,152
An inbound event, a sign-up, an
enrollment update, a consent change

162
00:09:26,398 --> 00:09:27,795
arrives at the integration layer.

163
00:09:28,391 --> 00:09:31,699
That layer asks the control plane,
"For this integration and this

164
00:09:31,712 --> 00:09:33,819
records cohort, where does this go?

165
00:09:34,435 --> 00:09:36,147
Legacy, new, or both?"

166
00:09:36,813 --> 00:09:39,561
This sounds simple, but
there is an important catch.

167
00:09:40,081 --> 00:09:43,912
The moment you can route to two
places or switches, switch routes

168
00:09:44,089 --> 00:09:48,931
midstream, you have built a distributed
system with all the usual problems.

169
00:09:49,716 --> 00:09:51,418
So a few non-negotiables here.

170
00:09:52,043 --> 00:09:56,113
Every message carries a correlation ID
from the moment it enters, so you can

171
00:09:56,113 --> 00:09:58,209
trace it across both the platforms.

172
00:09:58,883 --> 00:10:04,242
Writes are idempotent, keyed so a retry
or replay can't create a second record.

173
00:10:04,939 --> 00:10:08,363
Duplicate detection runs on business
keys, not just technical IDs.

174
00:10:09,022 --> 00:10:13,263
Retries are bounded and land in a dead
letter queue someone actually watches.

175
00:10:13,741 --> 00:10:17,740
And a reconciliation store
can tell you per record which

176
00:10:17,747 --> 00:10:19,587
platform received what and when.

177
00:10:20,379 --> 00:10:24,268
Get those right, and switching an
integrations route become, becomes a

178
00:10:24,271 --> 00:10:26,382
configuration change instead of a cut-over

179
00:10:27,382 --> 00:10:30,026
Pattern number three:
shadow mode and dual run.

180
00:10:30,970 --> 00:10:35,576
A real production event arrives,
legacy processes it exactly as it

181
00:10:35,577 --> 00:10:39,337
always has, and legacy's result
is what the person experiences.

182
00:10:40,069 --> 00:10:44,502
At the same time, the new platform
processes the same event in shadow.

183
00:10:45,222 --> 00:10:50,100
It- its output goes to a
comparator, never to a customer.

184
00:10:50,383 --> 00:10:54,381
The comparator ask, "Did both
platforms reach the same conclusion?

185
00:10:54,749 --> 00:10:58,044
Same matched identity, same
segment membership, same consent

186
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:00,639
outcome, same next communication?"

187
00:11:01,303 --> 00:11:02,961
This idea has a long lineage.

188
00:11:03,344 --> 00:11:07,701
GitHub o- open-sourced a library called
Scientist that does this inside code.

189
00:11:08,426 --> 00:11:10,242
Run the old path and the new one.

190
00:11:10,530 --> 00:11:12,817
Return the old result,
record the differences.

191
00:11:13,186 --> 00:11:15,858
Sam Newman calls the broader
technique a parallel run.

192
00:11:16,376 --> 00:11:17,684
Two hard-won lessons.

193
00:11:18,307 --> 00:11:21,352
First, shadow mode must
suppress side effects.

194
00:11:21,754 --> 00:11:24,869
A shadow platform that actually
sends an email isn't shadowing.

195
00:11:25,829 --> 00:11:27,596
It's a second production system.

196
00:11:28,285 --> 00:11:30,241
Second, not every difference is a bug.

197
00:11:30,523 --> 00:11:32,992
Sometimes the new platform
is right and the legacy has

198
00:11:32,994 --> 00:11:34,336
been quietly wrong for years.

199
00:11:34,818 --> 00:11:39,153
That's valuable, but someone has to decide
which answer is the intended behavior.

200
00:11:39,736 --> 00:11:44,391
This is where hidden assumptions surface
before any p- any real person is affected

201
00:11:45,391 --> 00:11:50,353
Every pattern so far quietly relies on
one thing, knowing whether it's working.

202
00:11:51,016 --> 00:11:53,599
A migration flag should
never be just on or off.

203
00:11:53,812 --> 00:11:56,339
Every rollout decision
needs three companions.

204
00:11:56,795 --> 00:12:01,814
Telemetry, what we measure tagged
with the flag state, so we can compare

205
00:12:01,839 --> 00:12:03,641
old path and new path directly.

206
00:12:04,313 --> 00:12:09,635
A threshold, the num- a number that
tells us we are healthy or we are not.

207
00:12:10,113 --> 00:12:14,216
And a rollback action, what happens
automatically or by a named person

208
00:12:14,575 --> 00:12:16,146
when that threshold is crossed.

209
00:12:17,176 --> 00:12:20,180
Flag, telemetry, threshold, rollback.

210
00:12:20,935 --> 00:12:24,564
If one is missing, you don't have
a control, you have a switch.

211
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,420
The measurements go beyond
the usual SRE signals.

212
00:12:28,275 --> 00:12:34,396
Yes, API errors and latency, but also
reconciliation mismatches, duplicate

213
00:12:34,396 --> 00:12:39,088
records, workflows that started and
never completed, message delivery

214
00:12:39,089 --> 00:12:43,232
failures, data quality exceptions,
and support tickets that say the

215
00:12:43,232 --> 00:12:44,830
new system shows the wrong thing.

216
00:12:45,732 --> 00:12:50,271
One practical tip here, OpenTelemetry
has semantic con-conventions

217
00:12:50,286 --> 00:12:51,928
for feature flag evaluations.

218
00:12:52,403 --> 00:12:57,084
Emit the flag key and variant
alongside your traces, and did

219
00:12:57,084 --> 00:13:01,185
it get worse after we flipped it
becomes a query instead of a meeting

220
00:13:02,185 --> 00:13:05,391
Regulated environments change the
rules, and honestly, they make this

221
00:13:05,391 --> 00:13:07,252
approach more valuable, not less.

222
00:13:07,594 --> 00:13:11,502
When your data includes personal and
health-related information, a flag

223
00:13:11,503 --> 00:13:13,283
change isn't just a release event.

224
00:13:13,718 --> 00:13:17,015
It changes where sensitive
data flows and who can see it.

225
00:13:17,526 --> 00:13:18,551
So treat it that way.

226
00:13:19,266 --> 00:13:21,706
Every change to a
migration flag is audited.

227
00:13:21,974 --> 00:13:26,420
Who, when, from what to what,
under which approved change.

228
00:13:27,108 --> 00:13:28,679
Consent travels with the record.

229
00:13:28,868 --> 00:13:32,137
If someone opted out in legacy
yesterday, the new platform honors

230
00:13:32,140 --> 00:13:34,263
it today, whichever path is active.

231
00:13:34,747 --> 00:13:39,193
Access control is equivalent on both sides
during co-existence, not just at the end.

232
00:13:39,968 --> 00:13:44,094
And you can trace any record's
lineage, which system held it in

233
00:13:44,094 --> 00:13:45,852
which state at w- at which time.

234
00:13:46,850 --> 00:13:50,627
Two cautions: keep sensitive
data out of flag evaluation

235
00:13:50,653 --> 00:13:52,277
context and flag telemetry.

236
00:13:52,477 --> 00:13:53,876
Use opaque identifiers.

237
00:13:54,962 --> 00:13:58,763
And bring your quality and compliance
partners in early so the control

238
00:13:58,765 --> 00:14:01,762
plane becomes part of your control
process rather than a workound-

239
00:14:01,809 --> 00:14:04,617
workaround it-- workaround to it

240
00:14:05,617 --> 00:14:08,050
Here is the part that rarely
makes the vendor slide.

241
00:14:08,733 --> 00:14:10,890
Flags are technical debt with a timer.

242
00:14:11,305 --> 00:14:15,387
Hodgson's article warns that
toggles multiply and add complexity.

243
00:14:16,031 --> 00:14:18,016
In a migration, the risks are specific.

244
00:14:18,390 --> 00:14:20,336
Stale flags nobody dares remove.

245
00:14:20,823 --> 00:14:24,156
Nested conditions where one flag
only matters if another is on.

246
00:14:24,790 --> 00:14:28,846
Inconsistent routing where the web
layer thinks a cohort has migrated

247
00:14:28,872 --> 00:14:30,285
and the integration layer doesn't.

248
00:14:31,094 --> 00:14:35,853
Configuration sprawl across the-- across
three tools, and hidden dependencies that

249
00:14:35,886 --> 00:14:37,869
only reveal themselves during an incident.

250
00:14:39,053 --> 00:14:41,890
The cautionary tale is Knight
Capital in twenty twelve.

251
00:14:42,419 --> 00:14:48,021
According to the SEC's order, a flag
that once activated retired code was

252
00:14:48,038 --> 00:14:49,822
repurposed for a new functionality.

253
00:14:50,414 --> 00:14:53,465
One server missed the new
deployment and the old code woke up.

254
00:14:54,015 --> 00:14:57,629
The firm lost over four hundred million
dollars in about forty-five minutes.

255
00:14:58,290 --> 00:15:00,921
So treat every migration
flag like a small product.

256
00:15:01,204 --> 00:15:06,451
It has an owner, a purpose, a created
date, a retirement date, documented

257
00:15:06,458 --> 00:15:11,824
dependencies, monitoring criteria, and
a rollback plan, and never use a flag

258
00:15:12,824 --> 00:15:16,514
Which brings me to the question I
would ask before any migration starts.

259
00:15:16,899 --> 00:15:18,164
How quickly can we stop?

260
00:15:19,002 --> 00:15:21,213
Not can we roll back the whole platform.

261
00:15:22,270 --> 00:15:24,704
By the time you need that,
you're already in trouble.

262
00:15:25,001 --> 00:15:29,468
I mean, if anything goes wrong, how
fast can we stop just that thing?

263
00:15:30,561 --> 00:15:32,303
That's capability-level rollback.

264
00:15:32,557 --> 00:15:35,776
Say the new platform is primary
and working well, except a new

265
00:15:35,784 --> 00:15:38,350
reminder workflow is creating
duplicates in one channel.

266
00:15:38,928 --> 00:15:40,415
You don't roll back the CRM.

267
00:15:40,549 --> 00:15:44,747
You flick one-- you flip one flag,
route that one workflow back to the

268
00:15:44,748 --> 00:15:47,833
legacy path, and everything else
keeps running on the new platform.

269
00:15:48,442 --> 00:15:50,981
Now, for that to work,
three things must be true.

270
00:15:51,546 --> 00:15:54,236
The legacy path is still
warm and receiving data.

271
00:15:54,697 --> 00:15:57,523
The kill switch has been tested
and actually flipped in a drill

272
00:15:57,596 --> 00:16:01,587
with known propagation time, and
the person on call is authorized

273
00:16:01,587 --> 00:16:03,427
to use it without calling a meeting

274
00:16:04,427 --> 00:16:09,023
If you screenshot one slide,
make, make it this one Map.

275
00:16:09,632 --> 00:16:13,521
Inventory capabilities, integrations,
data domains, and dependencies,

276
00:16:13,562 --> 00:16:15,475
and choose your unit of migration.

277
00:16:16,736 --> 00:16:17,294
Flag.

278
00:16:17,484 --> 00:16:22,048
Put each unit behind an explicit owned
control with a state and a way back.

279
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:23,160
Shadow.

280
00:16:23,622 --> 00:16:27,091
Feed the new platform real
inputs, side effects suppressed.

281
00:16:27,612 --> 00:16:28,131
Compare.

282
00:16:28,197 --> 00:16:32,052
Reconcile outputs and decide difference
by difference what is correct.

283
00:16:32,630 --> 00:16:33,027
Pilot.

284
00:16:33,543 --> 00:16:37,176
Make the new platform the system of
action for a small deliberate cohort.

285
00:16:37,710 --> 00:16:38,262
Observe.

286
00:16:38,686 --> 00:16:42,363
Watch telemetry against threshold you
agreed on before flipping anything.

287
00:16:43,428 --> 00:16:44,110
Expand.

288
00:16:44,251 --> 00:16:47,198
Grow cohorts and capabilities
through explicit gates and

289
00:16:47,199 --> 00:16:48,271
be willing to step back.

290
00:16:48,959 --> 00:16:49,524
Retire.

291
00:16:49,588 --> 00:16:51,870
The steps teams skip.

292
00:16:53,460 --> 00:16:56,173
Remove the flags, the routine
code, the dual writes, and

293
00:16:56,173 --> 00:16:57,740
finally the legacy path itself.

294
00:16:58,672 --> 00:17:00,543
Notice that it is a loop, not a line.

295
00:17:00,755 --> 00:17:04,326
Each capabilities goes around it,
and different capabilities can be

296
00:17:04,326 --> 00:17:05,829
at different stages at the same time

297
00:17:06,829 --> 00:17:08,349
Let's go back to where we started.

298
00:17:08,953 --> 00:17:11,319
The riskiest feature you'd
rather ever release is a whole

299
00:17:11,331 --> 00:17:14,971
platform, but it does not have to
be released at all, all at once.

300
00:17:15,502 --> 00:17:20,340
A platform migration can be a series of
small, observable, reversible decisions.

301
00:17:20,904 --> 00:17:23,551
Three tings-- Three things
to take with, with you.

302
00:17:23,621 --> 00:17:27,841
One, treat migration as a progressive
delivery and separate deploying that,

303
00:17:28,305 --> 00:17:29,853
the platform from activating it.

304
00:17:30,351 --> 00:17:34,891
Two, use flags as a control plane
to shrink blast radius by cohort,

305
00:17:35,069 --> 00:17:36,786
capability, channel, or integration.

306
00:17:37,581 --> 00:17:41,995
Three, design rollback and
obser-observ-observability before rollout.

307
00:17:42,496 --> 00:17:46,580
and plan every flag's retirement
the day you create it.

308
00:17:47,533 --> 00:17:51,329
The best migration isn't a heroic
go-live weekend, it's one where

309
00:17:51,355 --> 00:17:53,020
no single weekend can hurt you.

310
00:17:54,596 --> 00:17:56,823
I hope you enjoyed the presentation.

311
00:17:57,823 --> 00:17:59,913
Feel free to connect with me on LinkedIn.

312
00:18:00,372 --> 00:18:00,808
Thank you.

